1. cpp source code 

2. preprocessed file g++ -E .cpp -o .i
The preprocessor expands:
All #include headers
All #define macros
Removes comments
Handles #ifdef conditions
➡️ Output: A pure C++ source with no directives, only expanded code.

3.  Compilation g++ -S .i -o .s
Lexical Analysis → breaks code into tokens
Syntax Analysis → builds AST (Abstract Syntax Tree)
Semantic Analysis → checks type correctness
Optimization → simplifies constant operations, inlines, etc.
Code Generation → emits assembly (.s)

4. Assembling g++ -c .s -o -o 
Assembler converts your assembly to machine code (binary opcodes), producing

5. Linking g++ -o -o .exe
Combines: 
All .o files (from your project and libraries)
Standard libraries (like libstdc++)
Resolves all function calls and global variable references

6. Loading  ./.exe
The OS loader:
Maps .text, .rodata, .data, .bss into virtual memory
Sets up stack and heap
Initializes runtime (like constructors for globals)
Jumps to main()

7. Execution 
Now the CPU executes instructions from .text, using:
.data / .bss for globals
Stack for locals
Heap for dynamic allocations
When the program ends:
Stack is auto-freed
Heap memory (from new) is freed by destructors or unique_ptr
OS cleans everything on process exit